We start with the data block, where we declare the number of observations \(T\), the number of states \(m\) and the observations \(\mathbf{y}\). Stan is statically typed, so every variable must be declared together with its type. Here \(T\) and \(m\) are integers and \(\mathbf{y}\) is an array of \(T\) real numbers. The constraint &lt;lower=1&gt; states that \(T\) and \(m\) must be at least one. In the data block, constraints act as checks, so Stan stops with an error if the data passed to the program do not satisfy them. Note also that the names declared in this block must match exactly the keys of the dictionary passed from Python.
data {
  int&lt;lower=1&gt; T;   // number of observations
  int&lt;lower=1&gt; m;   // number of states
  array[T] real y;  // observations
}
The parameters block declares the unknown quantities whose posterior distribution we want to explore. Here we declare the means \(\mu_j\) and standard deviations \(\sigma_j\) of the state-dependent distributions, and the rows \(\boldsymbol{\gamma}_i\) of the transition probability matrix \(\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\). Unlike in the data block, constraints here define the space explored by the sampler. The constraint &lt;lower=0&gt; restricts each \(\sigma_j\) to positive values, and the type simplex[m] restricts each row of \(\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\) to the probability simplex \(\Delta^{m-1}\) defined in Section 2.1, so that its entries are non-negative and sum to one. Stan enforces these constraints internally by transforming the parameters to an unconstrained space. Note that the initial distribution \(\boldsymbol{\delta}\) is not declared here, since under the stationarity assumption it is a deterministic function of \(\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\) and is computed in the next block.
parameters {
  array[m] real mu;              // means of the state-dependent distributions
  array[m] real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma;  // standard deviations of the state-dependent distributions
  array[m] simplex[m] gamma;     // rows of Gamma, gamma[i] is the i-th row
}
The transformed parameters block defines quantities that are computed deterministically from the parameters. They are recomputed at every iteration of the sampler and stored in the output, so their posterior distribution can be summarized in the same way as that of the parameters. Here we compute two such quantities. First, we assemble the transition probability matrix \(\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\) from its rows. Since each gamma[i] is a column vector while the rows of a matrix are row vectors, each row is transposed with the operator ' before being assigned. Second, we compute the stationary distribution \(\boldsymbol{\delta}\) using Equation 6. Instead of inverting the matrix \(\mathbf{I}_m - \boldsymbol{\Gamma} + \mathbf{U}\) explicitly, we use the right division operator /, which solves the corresponding linear system and is numerically more stable. The constraint &lt;lower=0, upper=1&gt; on delta does not restrict the sampler, as it does in the parameters block, but acts as a check. If the computed values fall outside this range, which can happen when \(\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\) is numerically close to a reducible matrix, Stan rejects the proposal and continues sampling.
transformed parameters {
  matrix[m, m] Gamma;                       // transition probability matrix
  row_vector&lt;lower=0, upper=1&gt;[m] delta;    // stationary distribution

  for (i in 1:m) {
    Gamma[i] = gamma[i]';
  }
  delta = rep_row_vector(1, m) / (identity_matrix(m) - Gamma + rep_matrix(1, m, m));
}
The model block specifies the two components of the log posterior in Equation 9, namely the prior distributions of the parameters declared in the parameters block and the likelihood. We use the following priors, which correspond to Equation 7 with \(\mu_0 = 0\), \(s_0 = 5\) and \(s_1 = 2\).

A \(\mathcal{N}(0, 5^2)\) prior for each mean \(\mu_j\).
A half-\(\mathcal{N}(0, 2^2)\) prior for each standard deviation \(\sigma_j\), obtained by combining a normal distribution centered at zero with the constraint &lt;lower=0&gt; declared in the parameters block.
A \(\text{Dir}(\mathbf{1}_m)\) prior for each row of \(\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\), stored in the array gamma.

These priors are weakly informative, since their scales are wide relative to the scale of the data, and they are the same for every state, so the prior is exchangeable in the sense of Section 2.3. No prior is placed on \(\boldsymbol{\delta}\), which under the stationarity assumption is computed from \(\boldsymbol{\Gamma}\) in the transformed parameters block.
Once defined the priors on the parameters, the likelihood is evaluated with the forward algorithm. Stan actually evaluates the log-likelihood and in order to undertand the code, we write the formula here
Once the priors are specified, the likelihood is evaluated with the forward algorithm. Let \(\alpha_t(j)\) denote the joint probability of the observations \(y_1, \dots, y_t\) and of being in state \(j\) at time \(t\). The forward algorithm computes these quantities recursively, and the likelihood is obtained by summing them over the states at the final time \(T\) (Zucchini, MacDonald, and Langrock 2016).
Since the product of many densities quickly underflows to zero, Stan works with logarithms, so the recursion is written in terms of \(\ell_t(j) = \log \alpha_t(j)\). The forward algorithm, however, requires adding probabilities, and the logarithm of a sum is not the sum of the logarithms. Given the logarithms \(x_1, \dots, x_m\) of several probabilities, the logarithm of their sum is
\[
\text{LSE}(x_1, \dots, x_m) = \log \sum_{i=1}^{m} e^{x_i},
\tag{13}\]
which Stan implements as log_sum_exp.
For the forward algorithm, we first declare the vector log_alpha and initialize it for the first observation
\[
\ell_1(j) = \log \delta_j + \log p_j(y_1), \qquad j = 1, \dots, m,
\tag{14}\]
Next, for \(t = 2, \dots, T\), we update each \(\ell_t(j)\) as
\[
\ell_t(j) = \text{LSE}\big(\ell_{t-1}(1) + \log \gamma_{1j}, \dots, \ell_{t-1}(m) + \log \gamma_{mj}\big) + \log p_j(y_t), \qquad j = 1, \dots, m.
\tag{15}\]
Finally, the log-likelihood is obtained by applying the log-sum-exp function in Equation 13 to the values at the last time point,
\[
\log L(\boldsymbol{\theta} \mid \mathbf{y}) = \text{LSE}\big(\ell_T(1), \dots, \ell_T(m)\big),
\tag{16}\]
and it is added to target, the variable in which Stan accumulates the log posterior density. Together with the contributions of the priors, target then holds the value of Equation 9 at the current value of the parameters.
model{

    vector[m] log_alpha;

    // Prior of the means
    mu ~ normal(0,5);

    // Prior of the deviation
    sigma ~ normal(0,2); // Restriction on sigma guarantees this is half normal

    // Prior of Gamma
    for (i in 1:m){
        gamma[i] ~ dirichlet(rep_vector(1,m));
    }

    // initialize forward log-weights
    for (j in 1:m) {
        log_alpha[j] = log(delta[j]) + normal_lpdf(y[1] | mu[j], sigma[j]);
    }

    // Verosimilitud
    for (t in 2:T){
        vector[m] log_alpha_new;
        for (j in 1:m){
            log_alpha_new[j] = log_sum_exp(log_alpha + log(Gamma[, j])) + normal_lpdf(y[t] | mu[j], sigma[j]);
        }
        log_alpha = log_alpha_new;        
    }
    target += log_sum_exp(log_alpha);
}
We have a Stan model and now we have to fit it. To fit the model from Python, we first load the simulated data and arrange them in a dictionary whose keys match the names declared in the data block. Note that the true hidden states stored in the file are not passed to Stan, since in a real application they would not be observed. We then compile the program with CmdStanPy, which translates it into C++ and builds an executable. Compilation can take about a minute the first time, but the executable is reused in later runs as long as the .stan file does not change.

from cmdstanpy import CmdStanModel
import pandas as pd

# Load and prepare the data
df = pd.read_csv("../data/02_hmm_2state.csv")

stan_data = {
    "T": df.shape[0],
    "m": 2,
    "y": df["y"].to_numpy(),
}

# Compile the Stan program
model = CmdStanModel(stan_file="../stan/02_ex1_normal.stan")

We then draw samples from the posterior with the sample method. We run eight chains in parallel, each starting from a different random initial value, and fix the seed so that the results are reproducible. By default, each chain performs 1000 warmup iterations, which are used to tune the sampler and are discarded, followed by 1000 sampling iterations. The summary method reports, for each parameter, the posterior mean and quantiles together with the convergence diagnostics \(\hat{R}\) and the effective sample size.

fit = model.sample(data=stan_data, chains=8, parallel_chains=8, seed=123)
fit.summary()

